  "result": "I added `click.KeyValue`. It turns a `key=value` string into a `(key, value)` tuple, and bad input fails with `'...' is not a valid key=value pair.` The 8 new tests pass, and so does the rest of the test suite. I couldn't run a mypy type check because mypy isn't installed here.\n\n```python\n@click.option(\"--set\", \"pairs\", multiple=True, type=click.KeyValue())\n# --set a=1 --set b=2  ->  ((\"a\", \"1\"), (\"b\", \"2\"))\n```\n\n**How it parses input:**\n- It splits on the first `=` only, so `a=b=c` becomes `(\"a\", \"b=c\")`.\n- An empty value is allowed: `a=` becomes `(\"a\", \"\")`.\n- Input with no `=` (like `ab` or an empty string) or with an empty key (`=b`) exits with code 2 and the required error message.\n- Spaces around the key and value are kept as they are; nothing is trimmed.\n- If it's given a 2-tuple that's already parsed, such as a default, it returns it unchanged.\n\n**Changes:**\n- `src/click/types.py`: the new `KeyValue` class, right after `UUIDParameterType`.\n- `src/click/__init__.py`: exports `KeyValue`.\n- `tests/test_types.py`: tests for valid input, invalid input, and use with `multiple=True`.\n- Docs: added an entry in `docs/api.md` and one in the built-in types list in `docs/parameter-types.md`.\n- `CHANGES.md`: added a new \"Version 8.6.0 / Unreleased\" section, since 8.5.0 is already released. The class docstring also says `versionadded:: 8.6`. Adjust both if this belongs in a different version.\n\nNothing has been committed.",
